/-!
# The order of the permutation model of `M₂₄`

This file computes the order of the permutation model `TauCeti.Sporadic.M24` of the Mathieu group
`M₂₄`, the subgroup of `Equiv.Perm (Fin 24)` generated by the two permutations `M24.a` and `M24.b`
of the ATLAS files `M24G1-p24B0.m1` and `M24G1-p24B0.m2`: it has
`244823040 = 2¹⁰ · 3³ · 5 · 7 · 11 · 23` elements.

Along the base `0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6` the model has basic orbits of lengths `24`, `23`, `22`, `21`,
`20`, `16` and `3`. The first five are those of a `5`-transitive group; the pointwise stabilizer of
`0, 1, 2, 3, 4` has order `48`, with orbits of lengths `3` (the points `10, 16, 23`, which complete
`0, …, 4` to an octad of the Steiner system `S(5, 8, 24)`) and `16` on the remaining points. For
each `i < 7` an explicit list of elements of the model fixes `0, …, i - 1` and sends `i` to each
point of its basic orbit. The two generators, and every product `s * r` of an element `s` of the
`j`-th list with an element `r` of the `i`-th list for `i ≤ j`, sift through these lists from the
`i`-th on, which by `Subgroup.natCard_eq_prod_length_of_siftCheck` pins the order to the product of
their lengths. The same argument shows that `0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6` is a base: only the identity of
the model fixes these seven points.

The sifts are run by `decide` in the kernel. To keep them cheap, every listed element is a product
of at most three of fourteen permutations given by tables of images: the two ATLAS generators and
twelve further elements of the model, each checked to be a word in the tables of the previous level
of the stabilizer chain. Through the surjection `TauCeti.Sporadic.exists_surjective_M24`, the group
presented by the ATLAS presentation `M24G1-P1` has at least `244823040` elements whenever it is
finite.

## Main declarations

* `TauCeti.Sporadic.card_M24`: the model has `244823040` elements.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.M24.stabilizerChain_seven_eq_bot`: the points `0, …, 6` form a base.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.le_natCard_group_M24`: the presented group, when finite, has at least
  `244823040` elements.

## References

* R. A. Wilson, R. A. Parker, J. N. Bray et al., *ATLAS of Finite Group Representations*,
  version 3, representation `M24G1-p24B0`, <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/>; group
  order on the page <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/spor/M24/>.
* C. C. Sims, *Computational methods in the study of permutation groups*, in Computational
  Problems in Abstract Algebra, Pergamon, 1970, 169–183.
* Á. Seress, *Permutation Group Algorithms*, Cambridge Tracts in Mathematics 152, Cambridge
  University Press, 2003, §4.1–§4.2.
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti.Sporadic

namespace M24

/- The kernel evaluates a permutation given by its tables of images and inverse images much faster
than a product of cycles or a long word, so the sifting computations below run on such tables: the
two ATLAS generators, and twelve further elements of `M24`, those of level `i` fixing
`0, …, i - 1`. Each table of level `i ≥ 1` is checked once to be a word in the tables of level
`i - 1` (and those of level `1` in the ATLAS generators), and the representatives along the base
are products of at most three of these fourteen tables and their inverses, so that sifting stays
cheap. -/

/-- The generator `M24.a`, given by its table of images; it is an involution. -/
